WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.890 --> 00:00:12.640
- Let's sort of summarize
what we talked about.

00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:16.670
I know there's a lot and there's
a lot more to talk about.

00:00:16.670 --> 00:00:19.500
So I also included some
resources on the page,

00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:21.200
if you wanna kind of
read more about data Viz

00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:25.440
and linking as well to some
of my favorite color Viz

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:27.070
that is particularly inspiring

00:00:27.070 --> 00:00:28.300
and just does a phenomenal job

00:00:28.300 --> 00:00:30.800
of communicating different
aspects of the disease.

00:00:32.010 --> 00:00:33.810
One of the biggest things we've seen

00:00:33.810 --> 00:00:35.310
and I kind of kept harping on

00:00:36.330 --> 00:00:38.150
is the

00:00:38.150 --> 00:00:42.040
audience's responses to a visualization.

00:00:42.040 --> 00:00:43.740
There's a lot we can't control

00:00:43.740 --> 00:00:46.300
with how an audience is gonna perceive

00:00:46.300 --> 00:00:48.450
or any given person is
gonna perceive a Viz

00:00:49.324 --> 00:00:51.590
but it's the responsibility
to the designer to make sure

00:00:51.590 --> 00:00:53.480
that the viewer has as much information

00:00:53.480 --> 00:00:56.950
as possible and has
clear means as possible

00:00:59.220 --> 00:01:01.563
to actually understand the graphic.

00:01:02.430 --> 00:01:04.800
So much, like I feel like in science

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:06.990
we always talk about
alternative hypothesis

00:01:06.990 --> 00:01:11.310
but alternative interpretations
are a huge part of data Viz.

00:01:11.310 --> 00:01:14.390
So you might come into
a graphic thinking like,

00:01:14.390 --> 00:01:16.410
well this is the way
you would interpret it

00:01:16.410 --> 00:01:18.020
but it's your job to think like,

00:01:18.020 --> 00:01:19.930
how else can this be interpreted

00:01:19.930 --> 00:01:21.020
by someone who doesn't know
anything about the data

00:01:21.020 --> 00:01:24.283
and it's just looking at my graphic?

00:01:25.910 --> 00:01:27.680
The other aspect I've touched on as well

00:01:27.680 --> 00:01:28.860
to accessible design.

00:01:28.860 --> 00:01:31.170
So very simple things like

00:01:32.920 --> 00:01:33.860
using color schemes

00:01:33.860 --> 00:01:36.640
that are accessible for
people who are color blind

00:01:36.640 --> 00:01:38.306
or even just something simple as like

00:01:38.306 --> 00:01:40.360
making your font size big enough

00:01:41.490 --> 00:01:44.590
is really critical for
people actually being able

00:01:44.590 --> 00:01:45.900
to understand your Viz

00:01:45.900 --> 00:01:49.466
and is that's a very I
think easy thing to do

00:01:49.466 --> 00:01:50.613
if you're making Viz.

00:01:52.780 --> 00:01:55.630
Much more difficult to deal
with is is cognitive bias.

00:01:55.630 --> 00:01:57.060
So I hinted at this a bit,

00:01:57.060 --> 00:01:58.520
but

00:01:58.520 --> 00:02:01.020
the problem with a lot of COVID Viz

00:02:01.020 --> 00:02:02.550
is people are coming in especially

00:02:02.550 --> 00:02:04.970
because it's become so
politicized unfortunately.

00:02:04.970 --> 00:02:07.080
People are coming into looking at Viz

00:02:07.080 --> 00:02:09.370
with their own preconceived notions.

00:02:09.370 --> 00:02:10.317
This applies to pretty much everything

00:02:10.317 --> 00:02:11.803
but especially for COVID,

00:02:12.920 --> 00:02:13.753
people come in thinking like,

00:02:13.753 --> 00:02:15.940
well, I feel like it's not that bad.

00:02:15.940 --> 00:02:17.890
You know, if you're
gonna come into looking

00:02:17.890 --> 00:02:21.530
at a graph already kind of thinking like

00:02:21.530 --> 00:02:25.120
this disease can't hurt me or
I'm invincible in some way,

00:02:25.120 --> 00:02:26.920
or I already got some immune.

00:02:26.920 --> 00:02:29.840
Whatever myths you wanna throw out there

00:02:31.430 --> 00:02:32.530
that's going to be the framing

00:02:32.530 --> 00:02:35.800
in with which you approach a Viz.

00:02:35.800 --> 00:02:38.830
So the visualization designer job

00:02:38.830 --> 00:02:41.370
is not to fight that cognitive bias,

00:02:41.370 --> 00:02:43.700
but it is to present, you
know, as much information.,

00:02:43.700 --> 00:02:47.110
so there's less fodder for that to be

00:02:49.500 --> 00:02:52.500
kind of latched onto
and re reappropriated.

00:02:52.500 --> 00:02:53.900
Of course, you can't control everything

00:02:53.900 --> 00:02:54.953
stuff's gonna happen.

00:02:56.710 --> 00:02:57.850
Grifters are gonna grift,

00:02:57.850 --> 00:03:01.660
but you know you have to keep that in mind

00:03:01.660 --> 00:03:05.183
for how people can can
take things out of context.

00:03:06.690 --> 00:03:07.870
And the flip side too, right?

00:03:07.870 --> 00:03:09.390
I think we often think about

00:03:09.390 --> 00:03:13.240
that more kind of anti-science perspective

00:03:13.240 --> 00:03:16.600
but people can also come into
things thinking, you know

00:03:16.600 --> 00:03:19.990
this is how I've been told
this is supposed to work,

00:03:19.990 --> 00:03:22.700
so that's the way it's gonna be.

00:03:22.700 --> 00:03:24.880
Even if it's something like, I don't know

00:03:24.880 --> 00:03:27.590
like for a long time where
there was very kind of

00:03:27.590 --> 00:03:30.780
a conflicting data about the
spread of COVID in schools

00:03:30.780 --> 00:03:32.130
it's still kind of unclear.

00:03:33.130 --> 00:03:34.330
And a lot of people
went in and being like,

00:03:34.330 --> 00:03:36.990
well, if it spreads here
is gonna spread everywhere

00:03:36.990 --> 00:03:39.810
or kids can spread it
more easily than adults

00:03:39.810 --> 00:03:41.310
and all kinds of narratives, right?

00:03:41.310 --> 00:03:45.220
Like lots of quick distillations
to get clear pictures

00:03:45.220 --> 00:03:48.280
of course, visualizing things like this

00:03:49.560 --> 00:03:51.070
is extra precarious

00:03:51.070 --> 00:03:54.810
because the data upon
which you're visualizing

00:03:54.810 --> 00:03:57.000
often is conflicting.

00:03:57.000 --> 00:03:59.399
And so that's something to
keep in mind when choosing

00:03:59.399 --> 00:04:03.660
to make a visualization
for a piece of data

00:04:03.660 --> 00:04:05.113
think about the data first.

00:04:06.620 --> 00:04:11.343
And so this gets into another
point I wanted to touch on.

00:04:12.632 --> 00:04:14.910
I'm just gonna jump down
to understand the data

00:04:14.910 --> 00:04:17.543
because actually this is
really, really important.

00:04:18.880 --> 00:04:20.590
I showed many different types

00:04:20.590 --> 00:04:23.830
of data in these different graphics.

00:04:23.830 --> 00:04:25.193
Something as simple,

00:04:26.370 --> 00:04:29.480
a sensibly simple as COVID cases

00:04:31.490 --> 00:04:33.590
can mean so many different things.

00:04:33.590 --> 00:04:37.170
So the way in which a
COVID case is defined

00:04:37.170 --> 00:04:39.380
can depend on department of health.

00:04:39.380 --> 00:04:40.440
It can depend on

00:04:41.460 --> 00:04:43.460
the person running the test.

00:04:43.460 --> 00:04:45.770
It can depend on the country.

00:04:45.770 --> 00:04:49.700
Like there are so many different
kind of caveats to that.

00:04:49.700 --> 00:04:53.760
And so it's the absolute
responsibility at bare minimum

00:04:53.760 --> 00:04:56.230
for someone designing a
visualization to really take

00:04:56.230 --> 00:04:58.710
into consideration what the data

00:04:58.710 --> 00:05:01.190
that they're actually showing is.

00:05:01.190 --> 00:05:04.800
And so I'll get into this a
bit more for the coding video

00:05:04.800 --> 00:05:05.633
but

00:05:07.680 --> 00:05:09.350
when getting any kind of your data

00:05:09.350 --> 00:05:10.440
it's your job to understand

00:05:10.440 --> 00:05:12.540
like where did this data come from?

00:05:12.540 --> 00:05:13.720
Who collected it?

00:05:13.720 --> 00:05:15.080
How was it collected?

00:05:15.080 --> 00:05:17.192
What's the quality of the data.

00:05:17.192 --> 00:05:20.370
How are are metrics being defined

00:05:20.370 --> 00:05:22.120
or calculated and things like that?

00:05:23.330 --> 00:05:25.090
This is really like fundamental

00:05:25.090 --> 00:05:27.340
to making any kind of visualization.

00:05:27.340 --> 00:05:29.970
And oftentimes this is
the bulk of the work.

00:05:29.970 --> 00:05:31.150
If you don't understand the data

00:05:31.150 --> 00:05:33.360
you have no business
trying to visualize it

00:05:33.360 --> 00:05:35.563
just as a kind of fact.

00:05:37.450 --> 00:05:39.780
But oftentimes that's mostly work.

00:05:39.780 --> 00:05:41.350
And the actual creation of the graphic

00:05:41.350 --> 00:05:43.960
is maybe even less time,

00:05:43.960 --> 00:05:45.940
but that's a huge part of data Viz.

00:05:45.940 --> 00:05:49.130
And so the thing I've talked
about probably the most today

00:05:49.130 --> 00:05:50.410
is providing context.

00:05:50.410 --> 00:05:51.420
So

00:05:51.420 --> 00:05:55.120
it's really probably the biggest problem

00:05:55.120 --> 00:05:56.730
that visualization struggle with

00:05:56.730 --> 00:05:58.300
in that they're often missing

00:05:58.300 --> 00:06:02.773
or partially represented
to inject bias potentially.

00:06:04.000 --> 00:06:05.700
Or if it's inadvertent

00:06:05.700 --> 00:06:07.723
the bias is objected for them, right?

00:06:08.670 --> 00:06:11.040
And so using annotations
like I showed you some

00:06:11.040 --> 00:06:13.160
of the financial times graphics

00:06:13.160 --> 00:06:15.690
is really cool and very, very helpful

00:06:15.690 --> 00:06:17.420
for framing stuff for people, right?

00:06:17.420 --> 00:06:19.530
Like little arrows and lines to be like

00:06:19.530 --> 00:06:20.400
this is what this trend is,

00:06:20.400 --> 00:06:22.220
and this is what this label is.

00:06:22.220 --> 00:06:24.900
I know it's not very common
unfortunately in a lot

00:06:24.900 --> 00:06:28.500
of science Viz but it's really
good for general data VIz

00:06:28.500 --> 00:06:32.590
especially for something
as complicated as COVID-19.

00:06:32.590 --> 00:06:33.980
Explaining labels and terms.

00:06:33.980 --> 00:06:37.430
So saying something
like case fatality rate

00:06:37.430 --> 00:06:41.400
without saying what that even
means is really problematic.

00:06:41.400 --> 00:06:44.150
You have to say it like
this was calculated using

00:06:44.150 --> 00:06:46.653
this metric and using these definitions.

00:06:48.040 --> 00:06:51.370
And so yeah, that's very easy thing to do

00:06:51.370 --> 00:06:53.033
but a lot of people don't do it.

00:06:54.270 --> 00:06:57.120
Showing trends over time is
generally a good thing to do.

00:06:58.080 --> 00:06:59.410
So I mentioned how

00:07:00.440 --> 00:07:01.910
the Reuters Graphic

00:07:01.910 --> 00:07:04.010
was showing those weekly aggregations.

00:07:04.010 --> 00:07:08.170
A lot of other publications
we'll do seven day averages

00:07:08.170 --> 00:07:09.820
which is something I will use as well

00:07:09.820 --> 00:07:13.720
in the coding video as a
way to remove the noise

00:07:13.720 --> 00:07:15.240
from

00:07:15.240 --> 00:07:16.750
individual

00:07:16.750 --> 00:07:18.350
days being potentially off

00:07:18.350 --> 00:07:20.660
for randomly higher or low.

00:07:20.660 --> 00:07:23.330
And you can misinterpret maybe a data dump

00:07:23.330 --> 00:07:25.590
of cases as being like
an actual spike in cases.

00:07:25.590 --> 00:07:29.140
For example, when maybe the reality is

00:07:30.880 --> 00:07:34.040
the person inputting the
data took the weekend off

00:07:34.040 --> 00:07:38.240
and waited to input like a
backlog of data from two days

00:07:38.240 --> 00:07:40.160
on the following day.

00:07:40.160 --> 00:07:43.330
So that that's something that
happens a lot unfortunately

00:07:43.330 --> 00:07:45.760
especially in many many
different health departments

00:07:45.760 --> 00:07:46.593
in the country.

00:07:48.550 --> 00:07:53.420
But a one day number also just
showing someone like today

00:07:53.420 --> 00:07:56.650
this number of cases happened
is also kind of problematic

00:07:56.650 --> 00:07:57.483
because

00:07:59.960 --> 00:08:01.380
it doesn't really mean much, right?

00:08:01.380 --> 00:08:04.120
When you talk about a single day

00:08:04.120 --> 00:08:05.690
when you're not comparing
it with the day before

00:08:05.690 --> 00:08:06.623
or the day after.

00:08:07.990 --> 00:08:10.810
It won't help viewers
understand the bigger picture.

00:08:10.810 --> 00:08:12.960
And again reporting data issues

00:08:12.960 --> 00:08:15.630
can affect single day numbers.

00:08:15.630 --> 00:08:17.460
Another really important thing

00:08:17.460 --> 00:08:20.020
is to keep your metrics consistent.

00:08:20.020 --> 00:08:22.080
And it sounds really basic but

00:08:23.700 --> 00:08:25.340
you know, I showed you
that kind of like messed up

00:08:25.340 --> 00:08:28.900
why access before but mixing
different types of testing

00:08:28.900 --> 00:08:31.743
for example, is a common thing people do.

00:08:32.930 --> 00:08:35.160
That can be really misleading.

00:08:35.160 --> 00:08:38.840
Some data Viz have these
manipulated axes to create

00:08:38.840 --> 00:08:40.740
you know, maybe more extreme graphs,

00:08:40.740 --> 00:08:44.410
others have compared
viruses to things like

00:08:44.410 --> 00:08:46.290
the plague or Influenza

00:08:47.530 --> 00:08:48.363
but then do

00:08:48.363 --> 00:08:50.733
that over maybe like
disproportionate time lengths.

00:08:51.930 --> 00:08:53.280
So,

00:08:53.280 --> 00:08:56.200
like comparing six months of COVID data

00:08:56.200 --> 00:08:58.360
to a year of Influenza,

00:08:58.360 --> 00:09:00.618
doesn't sound like a huge
difference, but it actually is.

00:09:00.618 --> 00:09:03.417
That's double the time
with which, you know

00:09:03.417 --> 00:09:04.810
the disease can spread

00:09:04.810 --> 00:09:07.653
and you know, go around a population.

00:09:08.930 --> 00:09:13.330
So yeah, keeping comparisons
as comparable as possible.

00:09:13.330 --> 00:09:17.340
And that gets into as well the
sort of normalization thing

00:09:18.280 --> 00:09:19.930
for population as well, too.

00:09:19.930 --> 00:09:21.950
So keeping population

00:09:23.470 --> 00:09:26.459
as limited as a compound as possible.

00:09:26.459 --> 00:09:29.850
Population is important but
it's important in the sense

00:09:29.850 --> 00:09:33.590
in that it can help calculate
like per capita type changes.

00:09:33.590 --> 00:09:37.150
So when you're comparing States,
cities, regions in general

00:09:38.250 --> 00:09:39.650
the number of people matter,

00:09:40.560 --> 00:09:43.810
more than that actually the
people themselves matters.

00:09:43.810 --> 00:09:46.620
So demographics change as well too.

00:09:46.620 --> 00:09:51.190
So it's rare to see this but
it's actually good practice

00:09:51.190 --> 00:09:54.850
to probably correct for things like age

00:09:54.850 --> 00:09:57.530
and racial diversity to see.

00:09:57.530 --> 00:09:58.363
You actually,

00:09:58.363 --> 00:10:01.090
if you look for those things
and normalize across population

00:10:01.090 --> 00:10:01.923
that way

00:10:03.060 --> 00:10:06.540
you can start to pick out differences

00:10:06.540 --> 00:10:08.100
in

00:10:08.100 --> 00:10:10.790
maybe like severity of the disease,

00:10:10.790 --> 00:10:11.790
hitting different populations

00:10:11.790 --> 00:10:13.860
which is something that
actually did come out

00:10:13.860 --> 00:10:17.380
when the spread of COVID
was more carefully studied

00:10:17.380 --> 00:10:20.933
across different race groups in America.

00:10:23.750 --> 00:10:26.120
And the most important thing I think

00:10:26.120 --> 00:10:29.200
that is not so often done.

00:10:29.200 --> 00:10:32.160
I think that New York Times
piece did that really well,

00:10:32.160 --> 00:10:34.630
was to acknowledge the uncertainty

00:10:34.630 --> 00:10:37.293
and limitations of the data.

00:10:38.580 --> 00:10:40.090
So, right.

00:10:40.090 --> 00:10:41.490
I think understanding

00:10:43.080 --> 00:10:44.740
what we don't know,

00:10:44.740 --> 00:10:47.180
understanding what isn't shown,

00:10:47.180 --> 00:10:50.160
is something that is inherently
very, very hard to do.

00:10:50.160 --> 00:10:53.310
It kind of gets back to my
initial point earlier of

00:10:54.174 --> 00:10:56.330
kind of communicating a certainty

00:10:56.330 --> 00:10:58.470
is the most difficult thing to do

00:10:58.470 --> 00:11:00.420
especially because people are expecting

00:11:01.270 --> 00:11:05.143
clear cut simple straightforward answers.

00:11:06.120 --> 00:11:07.720
But this is the kind of

00:11:07.720 --> 00:11:10.870
of responsibility of the
visualization designer.

00:11:10.870 --> 00:11:14.000
So something like adding a note section

00:11:14.000 --> 00:11:15.737
at the end of the Viz,

00:11:15.737 --> 00:11:17.580
including some additional texts

00:11:17.580 --> 00:11:20.140
to kind of clarify some
points or saying like,

00:11:20.140 --> 00:11:22.930
oh on these two days, data is missing

00:11:22.930 --> 00:11:23.950
and so to handle that,

00:11:23.950 --> 00:11:26.560
we like removed these
days from the calculation.

00:11:26.560 --> 00:11:27.993
Easy thing to do.

00:11:29.450 --> 00:11:31.860
But the kind of scope of those limitations

00:11:31.860 --> 00:11:35.400
and the scope of the uncertainty
kind of as they grow,

00:11:35.400 --> 00:11:38.450
it makes it harder to actually make a Viz

00:11:38.450 --> 00:11:39.283
that's compelling.

00:11:39.283 --> 00:11:40.250
So that's also something you weigh

00:11:40.250 --> 00:11:42.317
when you even decide to to make your Viz

00:11:42.317 --> 00:11:43.503
in the first place.

00:11:44.930 --> 00:11:46.603
Making COVID Viz

00:11:47.790 --> 00:11:48.740
in this day and age

00:11:49.631 --> 00:11:52.060
is it's something that's
really important still.

00:11:52.060 --> 00:11:54.510
We're still very much the
midst of this pandemic.

00:11:55.530 --> 00:11:58.200
I thought the study was like pretty cool,

00:11:58.200 --> 00:11:59.120
they took

00:12:01.010 --> 00:12:03.940
a little over 5,000 COVID visualizations

00:12:03.940 --> 00:12:05.640
from April to May

00:12:07.002 --> 00:12:08.093
of this past year,

00:12:09.670 --> 00:12:10.960
specifically from Twitter.

00:12:10.960 --> 00:12:12.460
And they were about COVID-19

00:12:13.840 --> 00:12:17.340
and tried to look at potential challenges

00:12:17.340 --> 00:12:19.400
with how things were framed.

00:12:19.400 --> 00:12:22.410
And a lot of this is stuff
I just mentioned, right?

00:12:22.410 --> 00:12:25.353
Cognitive bias, not
really getting the virus.

00:12:28.140 --> 00:12:28.973
Yeah,

00:12:30.474 --> 00:12:31.940
these are problems that

00:12:33.600 --> 00:12:34.860
I think all data Viz

00:12:34.860 --> 00:12:35.823
has to deal with.

00:12:39.150 --> 00:12:42.910
And I think the fact that
mistrust is so high here

00:12:42.910 --> 00:12:43.830
just kind of

00:12:45.310 --> 00:12:46.143
highlights just

00:12:48.823 --> 00:12:50.150
how

00:12:50.150 --> 00:12:50.983
kind of messy

00:12:52.827 --> 00:12:53.660
the audience is

00:12:53.660 --> 00:12:56.130
in terms of putting in you COVID Viz.

00:12:56.130 --> 00:12:59.830
So many people are
distrustful of data sources

00:12:59.830 --> 00:13:02.590
and so being as transparent
and as explicit about where

00:13:02.590 --> 00:13:04.160
the data is coming from,

00:13:04.160 --> 00:13:07.660
is really, really key to
kind of conveying something

00:13:07.660 --> 00:13:09.177
with your Viz if you're
going to put something

00:13:09.177 --> 00:13:10.780
out there in the public

00:13:12.320 --> 00:13:14.220
The information

00:13:14.220 --> 00:13:17.520
that is in any given Viz sometimes

00:13:17.520 --> 00:13:21.230
like it's intentionally scared
and that breeds, you know

00:13:21.230 --> 00:13:22.793
that kind of mistrust.

00:13:23.820 --> 00:13:26.070
And so right, these are just some

00:13:26.070 --> 00:13:29.260
the top things researchers found as being

00:13:29.260 --> 00:13:30.640
like the biggest challenges

00:13:32.396 --> 00:13:33.960
with Viz

00:13:33.960 --> 00:13:34.930
on Twitter

00:13:34.930 --> 00:13:36.460
which is unfortunately a place where a lot

00:13:36.460 --> 00:13:39.433
of people share, share COVID Viz.

00:13:41.220 --> 00:13:43.370
Some of it's great and some
of it is very much not.

00:13:43.370 --> 00:13:44.930
So,

00:13:44.930 --> 00:13:46.600
it's oftentimes hard to

00:13:48.700 --> 00:13:50.233
parse those differences.

00:13:52.252 --> 00:13:53.810
And on an awesome note,

00:13:53.810 --> 00:13:57.513
there is so much like
phenomenal COVID Viz out there,

00:13:58.400 --> 00:14:00.590
like truly inspiring.

00:14:00.590 --> 00:14:02.240
This is just a small sample of it

00:14:03.270 --> 00:14:05.860
and the links will be on the page two

00:14:05.860 --> 00:14:06.693
but

00:14:07.750 --> 00:14:08.583
yeah,

00:14:08.583 --> 00:14:10.240
some of this I already showed
from the New York Times

00:14:10.240 --> 00:14:12.230
and the Washington post
the financial times

00:14:12.230 --> 00:14:14.330
but they all their teams have made

00:14:14.330 --> 00:14:18.193
incredible, incredible other stories.

00:14:19.110 --> 00:14:21.743
Visualizations, interactives.

00:14:23.530 --> 00:14:27.130
I wanted to also highlight
a couple other ones

00:14:27.130 --> 00:14:29.000
from the Reuters Graphics team.

00:14:29.000 --> 00:14:33.700
They did this really
compelling piece on one woman

00:14:33.700 --> 00:14:37.320
in Korea who seated their
biggest COVID-19 cluster

00:14:38.890 --> 00:14:39.770
this is in South Korea,

00:14:39.770 --> 00:14:42.590
and the kind of story telling flow

00:14:42.590 --> 00:14:44.740
of that piece was really remarkable.

00:14:44.740 --> 00:14:46.973
Even more kind of innovative.

00:14:48.440 --> 00:14:50.790
Shirley Wu did this.

00:14:50.790 --> 00:14:54.530
I worked with a team of people
to create a literal game

00:14:54.530 --> 00:14:57.260
to kind of simulate
different conditions around

00:14:58.150 --> 00:15:01.680
public health measures
and situations around.

00:15:01.680 --> 00:15:03.140
Again, sort of a fake disease

00:15:03.140 --> 00:15:06.740
but in a pandemic situation
people with the pandemic game.

00:15:06.740 --> 00:15:09.410
So there's like a lot of
really, really fun Viz out there

00:15:10.460 --> 00:15:12.400
and so don't feel like, you know

00:15:12.400 --> 00:15:15.170
you need to like replicate
anyone else's work or anything

00:15:15.170 --> 00:15:16.650
but it's really inspiring.

00:15:16.650 --> 00:15:19.650
And so if you wanted to take time to learn

00:15:19.650 --> 00:15:21.567
about how to do COVID Viz,

00:15:22.490 --> 00:15:23.610
I think looking at their work

00:15:23.610 --> 00:15:25.633
is a really inspiring place to start.

00:15:27.040 --> 00:15:27.873
And yeah,

00:15:27.873 --> 00:15:31.170
it's completely okay to make Viz

00:15:31.170 --> 00:15:32.610
on your own time

00:15:32.610 --> 00:15:35.600
and build kind of skillsets

00:15:35.600 --> 00:15:38.430
and get feedback from
others until you're ready

00:15:38.430 --> 00:15:41.160
to make your own
visualizations in general.

00:15:41.160 --> 00:15:43.060
It doesn't have to be of COVID-19

00:15:43.060 --> 00:15:45.978
cause hopefully COVID-19 visualizations

00:15:45.978 --> 00:15:50.093
won't be as prominent of a
thing in the next year or so.

